# Local development variables for `wrangler dev`. Copy to `.dev.vars`, which is
# gitignored and never deployed.
#
# There are three ways to run the server locally, in increasing order of what
# they need and what they prove.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Fully offline. No Cloudflare account, no key, no network.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the default and what the test suite uses. DEV_FAKE_LLM swaps AI
# Gateway for a stand-in that emits well-formed protocol output in chunks — it
# exercises routing, the Durable Objects, the event log, SSE framing, the
# streaming parser's chunk boundaries and the usage recording. It is not for
# playing: the writing is canned.

DEV_IDENTITY=dev@localhost
DEV_FAKE_LLM=1

# DEV_IDENTITY stands in for a signed-in user and only works while no real
# identity source is configured. Set the Google variables below and it stops
# being consulted, which is deliberate: a leftover dev identity must not become
# a way past a gate that is switched on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Local Worker, real AI Gateway. Needs an account and a token; costs money.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `wrangler dev` runs the Worker locally but its fetches go to the real
# internet, and worker/aigateway.ts talks to the gateway over plain HTTPS rather
# than through a binding — so this needs no `--remote` and no emulation. It is
# the cheapest way to find out whether the gateway path actually works, without
# deploying anything.
#
# COMMENT OUT DEV_FAKE_LLM ABOVE to use this. It is checked first, so leaving it
# set means the fake wins and the gateway is never called.
#
# CF_ACCOUNT_ID=your-account-id
# CF_GATEWAY_ID=the-gateway-name    # part of the URL; omit only if "default"
# CF_AIG_TOKEN=your-ai-gateway-token
#
# The gateway routes to a provider rather than being one, so Anthropic still
# needs paying: either store a key in the gateway (BYOK) or let the player
# supply their own. Without either, the failure comes from Anthropic.
# GATEWAY_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
# GATEWAY_FLASH_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2b. Local Worker, OpenRouter directly. Probably what you want for dev.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenRouter is already what browser play uses, so the key is one you have, and
# this needs no Cloudflare account and no gateway. Model ids are the same
# `provider/model` strings, so switching between this and the gateway changes
# nothing else about the game. Checked *before* the gateway, so setting this
# wins over any CF_* vars that happen to be around.
#
# Again: comment out DEV_FAKE_LLM above, or the fake wins silently.
#
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# GATEWAY_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
# GATEWAY_FLASH_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5

# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL points somewhere else entirely. With `pnpm fakeprovider`
# running, this exercises streaming, the usage chunk, cost and tier routing for
# free — the parts that otherwise cannot be tested without spending money.
#
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-anything
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8799/v1/chat/completions

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Local Worker, real Access. Rarely what you want.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting these makes the Worker verify real Access assertions, which a browser
# hitting localhost will not have — so every request 401s. DEV_IDENTITY is
# ignored the moment these are set (deliberately: that is what stops it being a
# production bypass), so this is only useful for testing the failure paths.
#
# ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=your-team.cloudflareaccess.com
# ACCESS_AUD=your-application-audience-tag

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Google sign-in, locally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Needs http://localhost:8787/auth/callback in the OAuth client's authorized
# redirect URIs (`pnpm preview` pins that port so the registered URI matches).
# SESSION_SECRET is not from Google — it is the key this Worker signs its own
# cookies with, so any long random string will do: openssl rand -base64 32.
#
# Setting these turns DEV_IDENTITY off. Comment them out to go back to it.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret
# SESSION_SECRET=a-long-random-string
